WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.952
This chapter covers capnography, the breath by breath measurement of exhaled carbon dioxide.

2
00:00:07.952 --> 00:00:17.591
We start with how it is measured, mainstream versus sidestream, the normal numbers, and how it confirms tube placement.

3
00:00:17.591 --> 00:00:20.993
Then the normal capnogram, phase by phase.

4
00:00:20.993 --> 00:00:27.716
Then sudden decreases and increases, and abnormal shapes, with what each one means.

5
00:00:27.716 --> 00:00:38.975
We finish with capnography during CPR: when to improve compressions, when to consider stopping, and how it signals a return of circulation.

6
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:52.188
Capnometry is the number, capnography is the number plus the waveform, and the capnogram is the waveform itself, measured by infrared absorption.

7
00:00:52.188 --> 00:00:59.261
A mainstream sensor sits at the airway, on the endotracheal tube, with a fast response.

8
00:00:59.261 --> 00:01:07.715
It is best for intubated patients, and on a mask or mouthpiece it needs oxygen at six liters per minute.

9
00:01:07.715 --> 00:01:14.626
Its problems are weight on the tube, added dead space, and secretions on its windows.

10
00:01:14.626 --> 00:01:20.967
A sidestream, or aspirating, unit uses a pump to draw a sample through a tube.

11
00:01:20.967 --> 00:01:28.446
It is best for patients who are not intubated, on a cannula or mask, from infants to adults.

12
00:01:28.446 --> 00:01:35.925
Its problems are secretions, water or a kink blocking the tubing, and a slightly longer lag.

13
00:01:37.333 --> 00:01:43.812
Use a calibrated capnograph, and the inspiratory value should read zero.

14
00:01:43.812 --> 00:01:51.911
End-tidal carbon dioxide runs one to five torr below the PaCO2, more with high dead space.

15
00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:59.920
Normal PaCO2 is thirty five to forty five, and normal end-tidal is thirty to forty three.

16
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:09.009
RCP 105 gives a normal arterial to end-tidal gap of two, and an acceptable five in critical patients.

17
00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:16.208
To confirm tube placement, waveform capnography is preferred, the gold standard.

18
00:02:16.208 --> 00:02:28.986
A colorimetric detector is an acceptable alternative: purple turning yellow over six or more breaths means two to five percent carbon dioxide.

19
00:02:28.986 --> 00:02:37.355
A brief change is less convincing, because the stomach can hold carbon dioxide after bagging.

20
00:02:37.355 --> 00:02:48.333
Capnography confirms the trachea, not the depth; it cannot reliably detect a mainstem tube, so use a chest film for depth.

21
00:02:49.733 --> 00:02:57.742
Chapter eleven adds volumetric capnography as the fifth of its methods for finding the optimal PEEP.

22
00:02:57.742 --> 00:03:09.274
It measures the average carbon dioxide concentration of each tidal breath, which the notes describe as the carbon dioxide eliminated per breath.

23
00:03:09.274 --> 00:03:13.599
Raise the PEEP step by step and watch that percentage.

24
00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:22.408
The optimal PEEP is reached just before the percentage begins to fall, because overdistension adds dead space.

25
00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:29.605
The RCP 203 figure labels the normal curve A, B, C, D and E.

26
00:03:29.605 --> 00:03:37.685
From A to B, the patient exhales pure dead space gas, so there is no carbon dioxide.

27
00:03:37.685 --> 00:03:45.284
From B to C, dead space and alveolar gas mix, and carbon dioxide rises rapidly.

28
00:03:45.284 --> 00:03:57.692
From C to D is the alveolar plateau, alveolar carbon dioxide, and the end-tidal value is read at the end of the plateau, point D.

29
00:03:57.692 --> 00:04:05.099
From D to E, the patient inhales fresh gas, and carbon dioxide drops to zero.

30
00:04:05.099 --> 00:04:16.834
The RCP 105 deck labels the same curve as phases one, two and three: dead space, the rapid rise, and the alveolar plateau.

31
00:04:16.834 --> 00:04:24.433
Hess adds that the plateau rises gently, and its peak represents alveolar PCO2.

32
00:04:25.833 --> 00:04:31.269
If the waveform and number disappear at once, think disconnection.

33
00:04:31.269 --> 00:04:39.835
A value that falls toward zero as the tube kinks means obstruction, and complete obstruction reads zero.

34
00:04:39.835 --> 00:04:47.330
A sudden, proportional drop means cardiac arrest, because the lungs are no longer perfused.

35
00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:53.178
A very low value right after intubation means an esophageal intubation.

36
00:04:53.178 --> 00:05:04.214
Hess says it is generally very low, but it can be falsely high after antacids or carbonated drinks, and that fades over a few breaths.

37
00:05:04.214 --> 00:05:13.933
One trap: a fall from dead space, as in a pulmonary embolism, is not improvement, so do not lower the ventilator rate.

38
00:05:15.333 --> 00:05:21.264
An abrupt rise during CPR means return of spontaneous circulation.

39
00:05:21.264 --> 00:05:31.058
An immediate rise after intravenous sodium bicarbonate is expected, because bicarbonate forms carbon dioxide.

40
00:05:31.058 --> 00:05:39.146
A rapid rise with succinylcholine or volatile anesthetics suggests malignant hyperthermia.

41
00:05:39.146 --> 00:05:46.334
A baseline that does not return to zero means rebreathing, or a circuit problem.

42
00:05:46.334 --> 00:05:58.465
A steeper, upsloping plateau means airway obstruction and uneven emptying, and the RCP 105 figure shows it as the bronchospasm tracing.

43
00:05:58.465 --> 00:06:04.755
Heuer describes a shark fin shape as acute partial airway obstruction.

44
00:06:04.755 --> 00:06:13.292
A trend up means hypoventilation and a trend down hyperventilation, confirmed with a blood gas.

45
00:06:13.292 --> 00:06:24.883
Hess also lists rises with fever, sepsis, seizures and COPD, and falls with hypothermia, hypotension, hemorrhage and a cuff leak.

46
00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:27.635
Pause and think.

47
00:06:27.635 --> 00:06:37.564
On a ventilated patient, the end-tidal carbon dioxide suddenly falls by half while the waveform keeps its normal shape.

48
00:06:37.564 --> 00:06:41.736
A colleague suggests lowering the ventilator rate.

49
00:06:41.736 --> 00:06:46.075
What could this mean, and should you lower the rate?

50
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:53.000
[6-second practice pause]

51
00:06:53.500 --> 00:07:03.673
A sudden, proportional drop means the lungs are less perfused, as in cardiac arrest, or dead space from a pulmonary embolism.

52
00:07:03.673 --> 00:07:09.696
It is not improvement in ventilation, so do not lower the ventilator rate.

53
00:07:09.696 --> 00:07:12.300
Check the patient's circulation.

54
00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:19.625
If the waveform and number had disappeared at once, you would think disconnection instead.

55
00:07:21.033 --> 00:07:34.393
Because it shows both the adequacy of ventilation and the effectiveness of chest compressions, capnography is now a standard of care during resuscitation.

56
00:07:34.393 --> 00:07:36.215
Three numbers matter.

57
00:07:36.215 --> 00:07:45.844
An end-tidal value below ten, or an arterial diastolic pressure below twenty, means improve the quality of CPR.

58
00:07:45.844 --> 00:07:55.040
Failing to reach above ten after twenty minutes of CPR can be used as one indicator to stop resuscitation.

59
00:07:55.040 --> 00:08:11.002
And a sudden rise to forty or more, with a palpable pulse, rising blood pressure, spontaneous breathing or movement, and a better saturation, signals return of spontaneous circulation.

60
00:08:11.002 --> 00:08:24.708
After that, the deck aims for a PaCO2 of thirty five to forty five and a saturation of ninety four to ninety nine percent, and warns against hyperventilation.

61
00:08:26.133 --> 00:08:26.986
One more.

62
00:08:26.986 --> 00:08:32.004
During CPR, the end-tidal carbon dioxide reads eight.

63
00:08:32.004 --> 00:08:36.834
A few minutes later it jumps suddenly to forty two.

64
00:08:36.834 --> 00:08:43.083
What did the eight tell you to do, and what does the jump suggest?

65
00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:50.000
[6-second practice pause]

66
00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:59.355
A value of eight is below ten, which means poor blood flow, so improve the quality of compressions.

67
00:08:59.355 --> 00:09:07.226
The sudden jump to forty two, forty or more, suggests return of spontaneous circulation.

68
00:09:07.226 --> 00:09:15.902
Confirm it with a palpable pulse, a rising blood pressure, and spontaneous breathing or movement.

69
00:09:15.902 --> 00:09:25.025
After return of circulation, avoid hyperventilation, and aim for a PaCO2 of thirty five to forty five.

70
00:09:26.433 --> 00:09:28.887
Here is the chapter in brief.

71
00:09:28.887 --> 00:09:34.811
Mainstream sensors suit intubated patients; sidestream suits the rest.

72
00:09:34.811 --> 00:09:46.742
End-tidal runs one to five torr below the PaCO2, and waveform capnography is the gold standard for confirming the trachea, but not the depth.

73
00:09:46.742 --> 00:09:53.597
On the normal curve, read the end-tidal value at the end of the alveolar plateau.

74
00:09:53.597 --> 00:10:06.121
A waveform that vanishes is a disconnection, a sudden proportional drop is arrest or embolism, and an upsloping or shark fin plateau is obstruction.

75
00:10:06.121 --> 00:10:20.083
In CPR, below ten means improve compressions, never above ten after twenty minutes may mean stopping, and a sudden rise to forty or more means return of circulation.
